This script uses OpenCV and EasyOCR to capture video from a webcam,
identify and read text from two specific regions (voltage and
temperature in this case), and log this data to a file.

It is designed to be calibrated for a specific, fixed-camera setup, such
as this case where we are reading data from an LCD screen and a separate
standard thermometer.

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{Getting
Started}}{Getting Started}}\label{getting-started}

Follow these instructions to get the script up and running on your local
machine.

\subsubsection{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{Prerequisites}}{Prerequisites}}\label{prerequisites}

Before you run the script, you'll need a few things set up.

\textbf{Hardware:} * A webcam connected to your computer. * \textbf{(Not
necessary)} An NVIDIA GPU with CUDA installed. The script is pre-set to
use \texttt{gpu=True} for much faster processing.

\textbf{Software:} * Python 3.x * The required Python libraries:
\texttt{opencv-python}{[}@opencv{]}, \texttt{easyocr}{[}@easyocr{]}, and
\texttt{numpy}{[}@numpy{]}.

\#\#\# \textbf{A Note on the Webcam (Optional)} This script will work
with any webcam that OpenCV can detect, including built-in laptop
cameras or standard USB webcams. The most important factor is a stable,
well-positioned camera.

For reference, the development and testing for this project were done
using a virtual camera setup:

\begin{itemize}
\item
  DroidCam{[}@droidcam{]}: An application to use a smartphone as a
  high-quality webcam.
\item
  OBS Studio{[}@obsstudio{]}: This software was used to capture the
  DroidCam feed and output it as an ``OBS Virtual Camera,'' which was
  then selected by the Python script.
\end{itemize}

Again, this specific setup is not required. It is just one example of
how to provide a camera feed to the script. Any simple webcam will work
perfectly fine.

\subsubsection{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{Installation}}{Installation}}\label{installation}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Create a Directory for Images:} The script is hardcoded to
  save debug images to an \texttt{img/} folder. You must create this
  folder in the same directory as the script for the program to run.
  \texttt{bash\ \ mkdir\ img}
\item
  \textbf{Install Python Libraries:} Install the necessary packages
  using \texttt{pip}:
  \texttt{bash\ \ pip\ install\ opencv-python\ easyocr\ numpy}
  \textgreater{} \textbf{Note:} easyocr{[}@easyocr{]} will also install
  PyTorch{[}@pytorch{]}, which is a large download and a required
  dependency.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{How to
Run}}{How to Run}}\label{how-to-run}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\item
  Make sure your webcam is plugged in and positioned correctly to see
  both your target readings.
\item
  Open a terminal and navigate to the folder containing \texttt{a.py}.
\item
  Run the script:

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\ExtensionTok{python3}\NormalTok{ a.py}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}
\item
  Two windows will appear: `Voltage' and `Temperature'. These show the
  exact (and processed) regions being sent to the OCR engine.
\item
  To stop the script, make sure one of these windows is active (click on
  it) and \textbf{press the `q' key}.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{Important: Calibration is
Required!}}{Important: Calibration is Required!}}\label{important-calibration-is-required}

This script \textbf{will not work correctly} without calibration. You
must adjust the code to fit your specific camera, lighting, and display
setup.

Here are the key sections you \textbf{must} modify in \texttt{a.py}:

\subsubsection{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{GPU
Usage}}{GPU Usage}}\label{gpu-usage}

If you \textbf{do not} have a compatible NVIDIA GPU, you must change
this line:

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\CommentTok{\# From:}
\NormalTok{reader }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ easyocr.Reader([}\StringTok{\textquotesingle{}en\textquotesingle{}}\NormalTok{], gpu}\OperatorTok{=}\VariableTok{True}\NormalTok{)}

\CommentTok{\# To:}
\NormalTok{reader }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ easyocr.Reader([}\StringTok{\textquotesingle{}en\textquotesingle{}}\NormalTok{], gpu}\OperatorTok{=}\VariableTok{False}\NormalTok{)}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

The script will run much slower on the CPU but will still function.

\subsubsection{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{Frame
Cropping}}{Frame Cropping}}\label{frame-cropping}

This is the most critical part. You need to tell the script exactly
where to look for your data.

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\CommentTok{\#Change the crop values according to requirement}
\NormalTok{voltage\_frame }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ frame[}\DecValTok{0}\NormalTok{:}\BuiltInTok{int}\NormalTok{(h}\OperatorTok{/}\DecValTok{2}\NormalTok{), :]}
\NormalTok{temp\_frame }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ frame[}\BuiltInTok{int}\NormalTok{(h}\OperatorTok{/}\DecValTok{2}\NormalTok{):, }\DecValTok{0}\NormalTok{:}\BuiltInTok{int}\NormalTok{(w}\OperatorTok{/}\DecValTok{3}\NormalTok{)]}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

Python slices are in {[}y1:y2, x1:x2{]} format (rows, then columns).

\texttt{voltage\_frame} is currently set to the top half of the entire
camera feed.

\texttt{temp\_frame} is set to the bottom-left third of the feed.

You must change these pixel/percentage values to precisely isolate your
voltage and temperature readings. The \texttt{Voltage} and
\texttt{Temperature} windows will show you what the script sees in these
cropped zones, so you can adjust the values and re-run until they are
correct.

\subsubsection{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{Image
Processing}}{Image Processing}}\label{image-processing}

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  \textbf{Temperature}
\end{itemize}

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\CommentTok{\#These values need to be calibrated acccording to requirement}
\NormalTok{gray\_t }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ cv2.cvtColor(temp\_frame, cv2.COLOR\_BGR2GRAY)}
\NormalTok{t\_lower }\OperatorTok{=} \DecValTok{50}
\NormalTok{t\_upper }\OperatorTok{=} \DecValTok{100}
\NormalTok{\_,thres }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ cv2.threshold(gray\_t,}\DecValTok{130}\NormalTok{, }\DecValTok{255}\NormalTok{, cv2.THRESH\_BINARY\_INV)}
\NormalTok{kernel }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ cv2.getStructuringElement(cv2.MORPH\_RECT, (}\DecValTok{11}\NormalTok{,}\DecValTok{11}\NormalTok{))}
\NormalTok{closed\_img }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ cv2.morphologyEx(thres, cv2.MORPH\_CLOSE, kernel)}
\NormalTok{pretemp }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ cv2.bitwise\_not(closed\_img)}
\NormalTok{temp }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ cv2.GaussianBlur(pretemp, (}\DecValTok{17}\NormalTok{,}\DecValTok{17}\NormalTok{), }\DecValTok{0}\NormalTok{)}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

\subparagraph{Key Values to Change:}\label{key-values-to-change}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \texttt{cv2.threshold(gray\_t,130,\ 255,\ cv2.THRESH\_BINARY\_INV)}:
  The 130 is the threshold value. This is highly sensitive to lighting.
  Adjust it up or down until the text is clearly separated from the
  background in the `Temperature' window.
\item
  \texttt{cv2.getStructuringElement(cv2.MORPH\_RECT,\ (11,11))}: This is
  the kernel size for the `closing' operation, which helps connect
  broken parts of a number. You may need to make it larger or smaller.
\item
  \texttt{cv2.GaussianBlur(pretemp,\ (17,17),\ 0)}: This is the kernel
  size for the blur.
\end{itemize}

Look at the \texttt{Temperature} window while calibrating. Your goal is
to make the numbers you want to read clear and solid, while removing as
much background noise as possible.

It is also preferred to hide the decimal point, either physically or by
further image processing.

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  \textbf{Voltage} In this case, the cropped voltage frame, is converted
  to grayscale before it is used for OCR. Make sure the voltage reading
  is visible in the voltage frame. It is also preferred to hide the
  decimal point, either physically or by further image processing.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{Data Collection
Methodology}}{Data Collection Methodology}}\label{data-collection-methodology}

The dataset is collected by simulating a \textbf{real-world thermal
change (cooling cycle)} to capture paired temperature readings.

\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{figs/autotrainer_schematic.png}
    \caption{Auto-Trainer representation}
    \label{fig:autotrainer_schematic}
\end{figure}

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\tightlist
\item
  \textbf{Setup:}

  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    Place both the PT100 probe and the reference thermometer inside an
    electric kettle, submerged in water.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Heating Phase:}

  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    Turn on the kettle and heat the water to its boiling point.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Recording Phase:}

  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    Switch off the kettle at boiling point.
  \item
    Start recording a \textbf{video} using the smartphone.
  \item
    Ensure \textbf{both the PT100 LCD display} and \textbf{reference
    thermometer} are visible in the frame.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Cooling Phase:}

  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    Continue recording the cooling process as the water temperature
    drops naturally.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Data Extraction:}

  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    The recorded video acts as the raw dataset.\\
  \item
    Extract frames and apply \textbf{OCR (Optical Character
    Recognition)} to detect readings from both displays.
  \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{Output}}{Output}}\label{output}

The script generates two types of output:

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \texttt{out.txt}: A text file that logs the detected readings. Every
  15 frames, if text is found in both regions, a new line is added,
  formatted as:
  \texttt{{[}voltage\_reading{]}\ {[}temperature\_reading{]}}
\item
  \texttt{img/} Directory: This folder saves voltageXX.png and
  tempXX.png images. These are the exact frames that were sent to
  EasyOCR. If you are getting bad readings, check these images to see
  why. They are perfect for debugging your calibration and fixing the
  readings.
\end{itemize}

\texttt{out.txt} is not perfect due to some instances of incorrect
character recognition. The data was manually updated and is saved in
\texttt{trainingdata.txt}
